README for the UNIX Pep/7 system, version 7.1.

December, 2002

This is a description of how to install a Pep/7 system under UNIX/Linux.

Files
-----
The complete system consists of the seven following files.  All of them
are ascii text files, which you can inspect with your favorite text editor.

1.  Assembler file (one file)
    asem.cpp - the program source file of the assembler
       The assembler uses file mnemon for the mnemonics of the 
       unimplemented op-codes.

2.  Pep/7 simulator file (one file)
    pep7.cpp - the program source file of the Pep/7 simulator
       The simulator uses file mnemon for tracing purposes.

3.  Pep/7 operating system (three files)
    os   - the source listing of the operating system
    os.l - the assembler listing output of the operating system
    os.o - the hex assembler output of the operating system.  This
       file is used as input to the burn-in utility

4.  Mnemonics (one file)
    mnemon - the four mnemonics for the four unimplemented opcodes

5.  The makefile (one file)
    makefile - script for the make command to build the project

In addition to these seven files, there is a directory named examples
that contains the assembly language source code for the programs in
the book "Computer Systems", second edition, J. Stanley Warford, Jones
and Bartlett Publishers, 2002, ISBN 0-7637-1633-2.

The book contains the complete listing of os.l in Chapter 8.  It
explains how the loader and interrupt handler function. You should
probably be a little familiar with that part of the book, as well as
with the chapter that introduces assembly language. If you ever damage
or destroy os.l or os.o, you can always generate them by running the
assembler on file os.  Hence, it was not strictly necessary to include
them in this distribution.

The files that end in .cpp are C++ files that compile and run with the
GNU compiler, gcc.

Building the project
--------------------
To install the project, simply execute the make command. By default,
it uses the script in makefile, which compiles pep7.cpp into an object
file named pep7, compiles asem7.cpp into an object file named asem7,
and then assembles os into an object file named os.o.

Using the system
----------------
Here is a UNIX session that shows how to assemble and execute Program 5.1
from the book.

===================
% ./asem7 ch05/prog.05.01
% ls examples
PrntMsg         prog.05.05      prog.05.11      prog.06.05      prog.06.11
prog.05.01      prog.05.06      prog.05.12      prog.06.06      prog.06.12
prog.05.01.o    prog.05.07      prog.06.01      prog.06.07      prog.06.13
prog.05.02      prog.05.08      prog.06.02      prog.06.08      prog.06.14
prog.05.03      prog.05.09      prog.06.03      prog.06.09      prog.06.15
prog.05.04      prog.05.10      prog.06.04      prog.06.10      prog.06.16
% ./pep7
31824 bytes RAM free.

(l)oad  e(x)ecute  (d)ump  (t)race  (i)nput  (o)utput  (q)uit: l
Enter object file name (do not include .o): examples/prog.05.01

(l)oad  e(x)ecute  (d)ump  (t)race  (i)nput  (o)utput  (q)uit: x
Hi
(l)oad  e(x)ecute  (d)ump  (t)race  (i)nput  (o)utput  (q)uit: q
% 
===================

The asem7 command has usage:

asem7 [-v] [[-l] sourceFile]

The -v option prints the version number of the assembler.

If sourceFile is present and contains no syntax errors, an object file
named sourceFile.o is created. The ls command above shows object file
prog.05.01.o produced from source file prog.05.01.

If the -l option is present, a listing named sourceFile.l is produced.

The pep7 command has usage:

pep7 [-v]

The -v option prints the version number of the assembler. Otherwise, a
prompt loop is entered that requests the user to 

(l)oad      Load an object program
e(x)ecute   Execute a program assumed previously loaded
(d)ump      Dump the content of a region of main memory
(t)race     Set the trace options
(i)nput     Specify file or keyboard input (default keyboard)
(o)utput    Specify file or terminal output (default terminal)
(q)uit      Quit the simulator

In general, students use their favorite text editor to create the source
programs. The object programs are student friendly, in that they are
hexadecimal text files that can be read or modified or even written
from scratch, thus bypassing the assembly phase. Chapter 4 introduces
machine language before assembly language and has a few problems
to write some simple programs directly in machine language. It is
instructive for students to experience the programming process in
machine language as it gives them an appreciation for what an assembler
must do to translate a source program.

You can even have your students modify the operating system.  Once they
understand the interrupt handler routines, they can modify the code in the
HEXO instruction to implement an instruction of your choosing.  To test the
new instruction, simply change HEXO in file mnemon to whatever mnemonic you
would like, assemble the new version of os, burn the new version into os.rom
as described above, and execute the simulator.  All the tracing facilities
are available with the new instruction.  Several possible problems of this
type are included in the text in Chapter 8.

Symbolic links
--------------
You can make symbolic links to the executables asem7 and pep7, for example
to put in a bin file. However, the working directory from which you
execute asem7 requires the mnemon file, and the directory from which you
execute pep7 requires both mnemon and os.o. It is simple to change the
source code to reference the absolute path names of where you have
placed mnemon and os.o. Then, the local copies of these two files are
not required.

Chapter 8 of Computer Systems provides problems that require students
to implement interrupt handlers by changing the operating system. In
those circumstances they can modify their local copies of mnemon and
os.o to complete their assignments.

